<title>USA: US, EU hold third day of meat rule talks - Glickman.</title>
<headline>US, EU hold third day of meat rule talks - Glickman.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. and EU negotiators met again Wednesday to try to settle a years-old disagreement that could disrupt transatlantic meat trade from April 1, Agriculture Secretary Dan Glickman said.</p>
<p>&quot;They (negotiations) are still going on as we speak,&quot; Glickman told reporters after an early morning speech. &quot;We have until April 1 to see if we can get something worked out.&quot;</p>
<p>Several hundred million dollars in meat, poultry, dairy and pet food products were at stake in the &quot;veterinary equivalency&quot; talks.</p>
<p>European Union has said it will implement new veterinary and sanitary controls begining April. The Agriculture Departmenthas said it would retaliate for any cutback in access to EU markets caused by the new rules.</p>
<p>Glickman said he was not taking an active role in the negotiations and declined to charactorize them.</p>
<p>Negotiators were said to be stalled on a number of issues on Tuesday.</p>
</text>
